= Syntax — Simple =

'''Syntax''' is the way words and other language parts are organized into larger expressions.

For example, English usually treats “The dog chased the cat” differently from “The cat chased the dog” even though both sentences contain the same main words. Their structure changes who did what.

Syntax studies things such as phrases, clauses, subjects, objects, word order, and dependencies.

Syntax is different from [[Semantics/Simple|semantics]]. Syntax is mainly about structure; semantics is about meaning. They work together, because structure often helps determine meaning.

Different languages organize sentences in different ways, and linguists use several competing theories to describe that structure.

